<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capetian House of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1032">1032</a>–<a href="/years/1361">1361</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capetian House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was a branch of the French royal dynasty founded by Robert I, a younger son of King Robert II, who received the Duchy of Burgundy from his brother Henry I in 1032. Its dukes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> until Philip I died without heirs in 1361, after which the duchy passed to the Valois. A branch descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a> founded the royal dynasty of Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Capetian House of Burgundy" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Arms_of_the_Dukes_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>, descending from Robert I, Duke of Burgundy, a younger son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-of-france">Robert II of France</a>. He lived from 1011 to 1076, and his brother was Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The house ruled the Duchy of Burgundy from 1032 to 1361. Besides Robert I, the notable members of the main line include Hugh III, Odo IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-duke-of-burgundy">Philip I, Duke of Burgundy</a>, together with Margaret of Burgundy, the first wife and queen of Louis X of France, and Joan the Lame, the first wife and queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The main line passed from father to son through Hugh I, Odo I, Hugh II, Odo II and Hugh III, then Odo III, Hugh IV, Robert II, Hugh V and Odo IV, who died in 1349. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-duke-of-burgundy">Philip of Rouvres</a>, who succeeded in 1349 and is numbered Philip I in the genealogy, was the last member of the house. He died childless in 1361, and the duchy reverted to his liege, who two years later created his own son the new duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, beginning the Younger House of Burgundy.</p><p>The house also had cadet lines. The Montagu branch descended from Alexandre, Lord of Montagu, who died in 1205, and held a number of lordships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> over later centuries, including Sombernon and Couches. Other younger sons included Louis, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, who lived from 1297 to 1316, and Robert, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonnerre">Tonnerre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry, Count of Portugal</a>, in 1093, and he was the father of the first Portuguese king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a>. The senior legitimate line went extinct with the death of King Ferdinand I of Portugal in 1383, but two illegitimate lines, the Houses of Aviz and Braganza, continued to rule in Portugal, with interruptions, until 1910, and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> until 1889. Through this branch the house achieved the recognised title of King of Portugal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Burgundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Robert’s son Henry, on becoming king as Henry I, granted it in 1032 to his younger brother Robert, who founded the <b>Capetian House of Burgundy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
